# Untitled

Cobalt-based hydroxide nanoparticles $@$ N-doping carbonic frameworks core–shell structures as highly efficient bifunctional electrocatalysts for oxygen evolution and oxygen reduction reactions
Shiqiang Feng, Cheng Liu, Zhigang Chai, Qi Li $(\boxtimes)$ , and Dongsheng Xu ()
Beijing National Laboratory for Molecular Sciences, State Key Laboratory for Structural Chemistry of Unstable and Stable Species, College of Chemistry and Molecular Engineering, Peking University, Beijing 100871, China
$\circledcirc$ Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany 2017
## KEYWORDS
core–shell structure, cobalt-based hydroxide nanoparticles $@$ N-doping carbonic framework (CoOHCat@NCF), bifunctional electrocatalyst, oxygen evolution reaction, oxygen reduction reaction
## ABSTRACT
The development of highly efficient and earth-abundant oxygen evolution/ reduction reaction (OER/ORR) catalysts is essential for rechargeable metal–air batteries. Herein, cobalt-based hydroxide nanoparticles $@$ N-doping carbonic framework (CoOHCat@NCF) core–shell structures have been designed as highly stable and efficient OER/ORR bifunctional catalysts. The obtained composite shows enhanced catalytic activities and excellent stability in alkaline media. In the OER, a high turnover frequency $(2.03\ s^{-1}$ at an overpotential of $0.36\mathrm{V}$ ), low overpotential at high current density $(100\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ requiring an overpotential of $\ 0.38\ \mathrm{V}$ ), and excellent stability $(100\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for one week with no activity loss) have been achieved. Furthermore, although cobalt species-based catalysts are known as good ORR catalysts, their hybridization with NCF obtained from metal organic frameworks successfully enhanced their ORR activities. The efficient activity of CoOHCat@NCF as a bifunctional oxygen electrocatalyst can be ascribed to the core–shell structures stabilizing the active catalytic sites and the porous shell structure favoring electrocatalysis-related mass transport.
## 1 Introduction
As one of the most promising sustainable energy storage systems, rechargeable metal–air batteries have received much attention due to their many advantages, such as high theoretical energy densities, cost-effectiveness, and low weight [1–3]. However, metal–air batteries face several challenges resulting from the inherently sluggish kinetics of the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER), which govern the discharge and charge processes, respectively [4–9]. The most active OER and
ORR catalysts under alkaline conditions are still based on precious metals, such as $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}$ [10, 11]. However, their performance cannot be maintained over prolonged periods under alkaline conditions, especially at relatively high current densities [12]. Furthermore, high cost and low abundance limit their widespread use [13]. Therefore, developing low-cost highly efficient bifunctional electrocatalysts from earthabundant elements is a great challenge for superior performance in metal−air batteries.
The design and fabrication of bifunctional catalysts based on existing OER and ORR catalysts is also important. Conventional approaches toward bifunctional catalysts involve the physical mixing of ORR and OER catalysts to form a composite catalyst, which usually suffer from low cycling stability due to significant electron transfer resistance [14]. To address this issue, recent studies have investigated depositing OER/ORR catalysts onto other catalysts or conductive materials to form hybrid catalysts, such as $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/$ graphene [15], $\mathrm{Co}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{y}/$ nitrogen-doped carbon (NC) [16], nitrogen-doped carbon nanotube (NCNT)/CoO-NiONiCo [17], $\mathrm{{Co_{3}O_{4}}/N}$ -reduced graphene oxide (rGO) [18], and $\mathrm{Co(OH)_{2}/N C N T}$ [19]. Although the above hybrid catalysts could offer moderate bifunctional catalytic activity, maintaining this activity is a major challenge, as the catalysts need to endure the different reaction conditions of ORR and OER repeatedly.
## 2 Results and discussion
Herein, we have developed a novel cobalt-based hydroxide catalyst $@$ N-doping carbonic framework (CoOHCat@NCF) core–shell structure as an efficient OER/ORR bifunctional catalyst, in which cobalt phosphate nanoparticles $@$ amorphous isoreticular metal organic framework-3 (CoPi@aIRMOF-3) is formed and then converted to CoOHCat@NCF by thermal treatment under an $\Nu_{2}$ atmosphere and activation in alkaline electrolyte. In this uniform core–shell structure, the cobalt-based hydroxide catalysts were entirely encapsulated in NCF, which hindered the agglomeration of particles and promoted its stability and OER/ ORR activity. This resulted in a high-performance robust electrocatalyst for OER, with a high turnover frequency $(2.03\ s^{-1}$ at an overpotential of $0.36\mathrm{V}$ ), low overpotential at high current density ( $100\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at an overpotential of $0.38\mathrm{~V~}$ ), and excellent stability 1 $100\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ for one week with no activity loss). CoOHCat@NCF also showed good catalytic activities in the ORR owing to the respective ORR activities of both the CoOHCat cores and nitrogen-doped NCF shells.
The CoOHCat@NCF core–shell structures were fabricated in four steps. Firstly, the cobalt phosphate nanoparticles (CoPi NPs) were fabricated through a hydrothermal route [20, 21]. Secondly, the CoPi NPs were coated with aIRMOF-3 to obtain a uniform core–shell structure (CoPi@aIRMOF-3) [22–24]. These coated particles were then annealed under $\Nu_{2}$ for ${1\mathrm{h}},$ during which CoPi@aIRMOF-3 was transformed into a Co-based phosphate catalyst $@$ metal carbonic framework (CoPiCat@MCF). Notably, the annealing process was beneficial for electrochemical performances. Finally, Co-based phosphate catalyst was treated with alkaline electrolyte to convert the CoPiCat cores into Co-based hydroxide catalysts (CoOHCat) and the MCF shells into nitrogen-doped carbonic frameworks (NCF) because zinc ions were dissolved into the solution.
The CoPi cores of CoPi@aIRMOF-3 particles were amorphous and produced no obvious diffraction peaks, with only broad “humps” caused by diffuse scattering found in the XRD pattern (Fig. 1(d)). Characteristic peaks at 1,030 and $600{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ in the Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra, attributed to the stretching modes of $-\mathrm{PO}_{4}^{3-}$ groups, confirmed the structure of CoPi [20]. The amorphous CoPi particles were crystallized by thermal treatment at $580^{\circ}C,$ as verified by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) and XRD (Figs. S3(c) and S4(a) in the ESM). The IRMOF-3 shells of the CoPi@aIRMOF-3 particles were also amorphous, as confirmed by the FT-IR (Fig. S3(a) in the ESM), XRD (Fig. 1(d)), and electron diffraction (Fig. 1(b)) analyses [27, 28]. TGA experiments showed that the aIRMOF-3 shells were stable below $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ as were the CoPi@aIRMOF-3 particles (Fig. S2 in the ESM). When the temperature increased beyond $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the aIRMOF-3 shells were destroyed and gradually converted into MCF shells.
When the CoPi@aIRMOF-3 samples were pyrolyzed at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for ${1\mathrm{h}},$ the aIRMOF-3 layer was carbonized. Meanwhile, the amorphous structure of the CoPi
NPs was maintained. The characteristic IR peaks of aIRMOF-3 disappeared after the annealing process (Fig. S3(b) in the ESM). The XRD pattern of the annealed samples showed no significant change, except a new small peak at $44.2^{\circ}.$ , which corresponded to the (111) lattice planes of metallic cobalt (Fig. 1(d)). Energydispersive $x$ -ray spectroscopy (EDX) was used to verify the elemental compositions before and after electrochemical treatment in alkaline electrolyte. The results indicated that elements $\mathrm{\DeltaP}$ and Zn were missing after samples were treated with alkaline electrolyte, which meant that Co-based phosphate in the cores had been converted into Co-based hydroxide and elemental zinc in the shells that had been removed (Fig. S1(d) in the ESM). When catalysts were soaked in alkaline electrolyte directly, XRD and electron diffraction results showed that the amorphous CoPi was converted into hexagonal $\mathsf{\beta{-}C o(O H)}_{2}$ (space group: $R{\bar{3}}c,$ , JCPDS file no. 30-0443, $a=3.183\mathring\mathrm{A}$ and $c=4.652\mathring{\mathrm{A}},$ ) (Figs. 1(c) and 1(d)). Furthermore, $x$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Fig. S5 in the ESM) was performed to obtain more precise chemical states for all elemental components $(\mathrm{Co},\mathrm{O},\mathrm{Zn},\mathrm{C},\mathrm{P},$ and N). Before the electrochemical test, high resolution XPS of $\mathtt{C o2p}$ showed a high-energy band $(\mathrm{Co}2\mathrm{p}_{1/2})$ and a low-energy band (Co $\mathsf{2p}_{2/3,}$ ) for $C\mathrm{o}^{2+}$ (Fig. S4(b) in the ESM).
Furthermore, a shoulder peak at $778.5\mathrm{eV}$ suggested the presence of metallic Co [25]. After the electrochemical test, the XPS (Fig. S5(c) in the ESM) of $\mathtt{C o2p}$ showed obvious changes, indicating that cobalt was present as $C\mathrm{o}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Co}}^{3+}.$ , but not as metallic Co. Metallic $C_{0}$ and amorphous CoPi were transformed into Co-based hydroxide. Co NPs are known to be oxidized in electrochemical reactions, and $C\mathrm{o}^{2+}$ can also be oxidized to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ (Fig. S6 in the ESM) [29–31]. Notably, this CoOHCat did not contain a pure phase of ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2},$ with both $C\mathrm{o}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ ions present after a certain time in the electrochemical test.
The OER activities were mainly evaluated using a three-electrode electrochemical cell in 1 M KOH solution at room temperature. For comparison with Co-based phosphate catalysts, some of the experiments were performed in $1\mathrm{M}$ phosphate buffer solution. Notably, the entire modified electrode was treated with plasma, which increased the amount of surface hydroxyl groups and promoted the formation of oxygen vacancies to enhance electrocatalytic activity [32]. As shown in Fig. 2(a), although ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}$ and CoOHCat@NCF samples had almost the same OER onset potentials (1.52 V vs. reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE)), the rising tendency of the current densities and the increase in potential for CoOHCat@NCF was much larger. Therefore, the overpotential of CoOHCat@NCF at $10\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is significantly lower $(340\mathrm{mV})$ than that of ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ $360\mathrm{mV})$ . The overpotential at a current density of $10\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ per geometric area is regarded as the benchmark of heterogeneous electrocatalysts for the OER [33]. Notably, one of the outstanding advantages of CoOHCat@NCF was its superior OER performance at high current density. For example, the overpotential at $100\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ was only $380\mathrm{mV},$ which was a remarkable value for OER performance and much lower than that of $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ . The Tafel slope of CoOHCat@NCF was only $38\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ (Fig. 2(b)), which was smaller than those of $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ $(70\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}})$ and ${\mathrm{Co(OH)}}_{2}$ $(62\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}},$ ). Therefore, both ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ and CoOHCat@NCF had better OER kinetic performance than commercial $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ . From the above comparison, we found that the NCF shells improved the OER performance of the composites at a large current density, rather than reducing the essential activities of the Co-based cores.
To understand the auxiliary effect of the NCF layer on the OER activity, the numbers of active sites and turnover frequency of every active site of pure ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ and CoOHCat@NCF were characterized using electrochemical methods. The number of active sites can be extracted from the slope of the linear relationship between the peak current of the $\mathrm{Co}^{2+}/\mathrm{Co}^{3+}$ reduction wave and scan rate (Fig. S8 in the ESM and Fig. 2(c)) [34, 35]. Figure 2(c) indicates that ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ particles $\prime{\cal T}_{0}=24.1\mathrm{nmol{\cdot}c m^{-2}},$ ) had more active sites than CoOHCat@NCF $(I_{0}=12.3\mathrm{\nmol{\cdot}c m^{-2}})$ initially, probably owing to inhibition of the NCF coating layer. However, the NCF shell was not a dense structure and the active sites would be gradually exposed when the electrode was immersed in the electrolyte. Finally, the active sites of CoOHCat@NCF increased to $33.1\mathrm{nmol}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ , which was even larger than that of the ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ particles. We then compared the turnover frequency (TOF) of different materials at a fixed overpotential of $360\mathrm{mV}.$ The TOF of ${\mathrm{Co(OH)}}_{2}$ particles was $1.14\ s^{-1},$ , which is higher than many other materials (CoPi: $1.4\times10^{-3}\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ at $410\mathrm{mV}$ ; FeNC sheets/NiO: $0.2{\ s}^{-1}$ at $350\mathrm{mV},$ ) [34], but smaller than that of amorphous ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ on gold electrodes $(2.1\ s^{-1}$ at $360\mathrm{mV},$ [36]. After testing for ${12\mathrm{h}},$ the TOF value of CoOHCat@NCF had increased from 1.81 to $2.03\ \mathsf{s}^{-1}$ , which was a remarkable value for the OER. Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (Fig. S9 in the ESM) showed that the core–shell composite had lower charge-transfer resistance, which helped improve the kinetic performance.
Interestingly, we noticed that on a large piece of glassy carbon (GC) CoOHCat@NCF (see Video S1 in the ESM) had more small bubbles than ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ (see Video S2 in the ESM), which suggested that NCF could limit nanobubble growth. This might be explained by the thin liquid film mechanism and related to the formation and release of nanobubbles [37]. As shown in the thin liquid film mechanism, a highly efficient gas electrode should allow the gas molecules to easily diffuse along the electrode surface and the electrolyte to efficiently contact with the thin liquid film at the electrode surface. When there was no NCF coating layer on the surface of ${\mathrm{Co(OH)}}_{2}\ {\mathrm{NPs}},$ the nanobubbles stayed at the electrode surface and occupied the active sites. As the nanobubbles grow, but remain too small to escape, the electrolyte cannot adequately contact the active sites on the electrode surface. However, this NCF coating layer can limit nanobubbles growing at the active sites owing to surface coverage and having its own small channels. The gas molecules can only grow at the surface of the NCF coating layer, and there were enough active sites for oxygen evolution even at a high current density $(100\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ .
In addition to the good catalytic activity, the CoOHCat@NCF also exhibited good stability in the OER. To test their Faradaic efficiency and stability, the $\mathrm{IrO}_{2},$ ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2},$ and CoOHCat@NCF particles were loaded onto carbon papers. The Faradaic efficiencies of CoOHCat@NCF and ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2}$ were about $99\%$ and $97\%$ , respectively (Fig. S10 in the ESM). This small $2\%$ difference might be ascribed to the auxiliary effect of the NCF coating layer. The results of gas chromatography indicated that the products were pure $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Fig. S10 in the ESM). The stability was evaluated at a constant current $(100\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ) using long-term chronopotentiometric measurements. Figure 2(d) clearly showed that CoOHCat@NCF was much more stable and active than ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ . The activity of $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ decreased gradually, while that of ${\mathrm{Co}}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}$ fluctuated seriously. The activity of CoOHCat@NCF showed no obvious drop, even after chronopotentiometric testing for one week, indicating its superior stability (Fig. S11 in the ESM).
The OER performances at different $\mathrm{\pH}$ values $(\mathrm{pH}7$ in phosphate buffer; $\mathrm{pH}13$ in 0.1 M KOH; pH 14 in $1\mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{KOH};$ were investigated to determine the $\mathrm{\pH}$ effect and reaction pathways. As mentioned previously, electrodeposited CoPi was a good OER catalyst under neutral and weakly basic $\mathrm{(pH<9)}$ conditions [38, 39]. However, under strong basic conditions, CoPi was transformed into ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2},$ which greatly enhanced the OER activity (Fig. S12(a) in the ESM). That meant that the real active sites under strong alkaline conditions were the ${\mathrm{Co-O}}$ centers in Co-based OER electrocatalysts [40, 41] and that the role of phosphate was only to transfer protons. This could be clarified using a proton-coupled electron transfer (PCET) equilibrium. Co-based phosphate catalysts are known to comply with the PCET mechanism under neutral conditions [42, 43]. We speculated that a similar OER pathway occurred in the alkaline electrolyte [44].
In this process, hydroxyl ions acted as the oxygen supply and proton acceptor, which would be more effective than those in neutral solution. Meanwhile, the $C_{0^{-}}\mathrm{O}$ centers acted as the electron carrier and active sites (shown in Fig. S13 in the ESM). In this circuit, NCF would accelerate electron transport, resulting in CoOHCat@NCF showing a high kinetic rate and TOF. Although CoPi would be transformed into ${\mathrm{Co}}({\mathrm{OH}})_{2},$ its amorphous structure would still help to promote the electrocatalytic effect for water oxidation, as indicated in previous work [40].
